course: biology
lesson: 189
title: Masters of camouflage
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Camouflage means looking like your surroundings so that hunters, or the animals you hunt, do not notice you, like a child in a green jumper hiding in a hedge during hide-and-seek.
explain: Some animals copy a thing: a stick insect looks like a twig, a potoo bird like a broken stump, a leafy seadragon like floating seaweed and an orchid mantis like a pink flower.
explain: Others change: a cuttlefish switches colour and pattern in less than a second, and a flounder matches the sea floor, while a decorator crab simply dresses itself in sponge and seaweed.
explain: Camouflage works only if you keep still and match what the hunter sees; a tiger's orange looks bright to us, but to a deer, which cannot see orange well, the striped tiger blends into green grass.
fact: The leafy seadragon has leaf-shaped flaps all over its body and drifts slowly among seaweed, and seadragon fathers carry the eggs on their tails until they hatch.
fact: Stick insects look like twigs and sway like branches in the wind, and the longest insect in the world is a Chinese stick insect over sixty centimetres long.
fact: The potoo sits still on a branch looking exactly like a dead stump, and even with its eyes shut it can see through notches in its eyelids.
fact: Cuttlefish change colour and pattern in less than a second using skin cells that stretch and shrink, and they can ripple moving stripes over their bodies, which may confuse prey.
fact: An octopus, a cousin of the cuttlefish, normally has eight arms and can change colour and blend into rocks and walls too.
fact: The orchid mantis looks like a flower, catches insects that come to it, and some insects are even more attracted to it than to real flowers.
fact: Flounders change colour to match the sea floor and bury themselves in sand, and as they grow both eyes move to one side of the head.
fact: Many moths rest still on trees looking like bark, and when factory soot darkened trees, dark moths survived better than pale ones.
fact: Tigers' stripes break up their outline in tall grass and shadows, deer cannot see orange well, and no two tigers have the same stripes.
fact: The owl butterfly has big eye spots like an owl's eyes that may scare off birds, and many butterflies have eye spots to confuse predators.
fact: The frogfish looks like a sponge or rock, waits for fish and swallows them quickly, even fish as big as itself.
fact: Decorator crabs stick bits of sponge and seaweed on their shells to hide, and they change their decorations when they move to a new place.
exercise: How does a leafy seadragon hide? | Leaf-shaped flaps make it look like floating seaweed | It buries itself deep in the sand and waits there all day | It turns invisible | It hides inside shells
exercise: Who carries the eggs of a leafy seadragon? | The father, on his tail | The mother, in her mouth | Nobody, they just float away | A crab that lives nearby
exercise: Why do birds often miss stick insects? | They look like twigs and sway like branches | They taste of old wood, so birds spit them out | They hide underground | They fly too fast
exercise: How long is the longest insect in the world? | Over sixty centimetres | About six millimetres | About six metres or a bit more | About six centimetres
exercise: How can a potoo see with its eyes shut? | Through notches in its eyelids | Through its beak | It cannot see at all | Through little holes in its feathers
exercise: How fast can a cuttlefish change colour? | In less than a second | In about a week or two | Only once a year, in spring | Only while it is fast asleep
exercise: How does a cuttlefish change colour? | Skin cells stretch and shrink to show different colours | It eats coloured food | It paints itself all over with its own ink, like an octopus | It swaps its skin
exercise: How does an orchid mantis catch food? | Insects come to it thinking it is a flower and are caught | It chases birds | It digs for worms | It waits for fruit to fall
exercise: What happens to a flounder's eyes as it grows? | Both eyes move to one side of its head | It loses both eyes | It grows a third eye | Its eyes slowly move underneath its belly
exercise: How does a flounder hide? | It changes colour to match the sea floor and buries itself in sand | It climbs onto rocks | It glows in the dark | It swims very fast
exercise: What happened to moths when factory soot made trees dark? | Dark moths survived better than pale ones | Pale moths survived better | All moths turned white | The moths painted the trees to match their wings
exercise: Why are a tiger's stripes good camouflage? | They break up its outline in grass and shadows | They scare the deer away so it can sleep in peace | They keep it cool | They show it is angry
exercise: Why does an orange tiger hide well from deer? | Deer cannot see orange well | Deer are blind from the day they are born | Tigers turn green when they hunt in grass | Deer look only up at the sky
exercise: What may the owl butterfly's big eye spots do? | Scare off birds that fear owls | Help it see better in the dark forest | Attract owls that come to eat it | Keep its wings warm on cold mornings
exercise: How does a frogfish catch fish? | It looks like a sponge, waits and swallows them quickly | It chases them over long distances | It shoots water at them | It uses a net of slime
exercise: Why does a decorator crab stick sponge and seaweed on its shell? | To hide from predators | To keep its soft body warm at night | To eat later as a snack only | To look pretty for people
test: In Finding Dory, Hank the octopus has only 7 arms and blends into walls. How many arms does a normal octopus have? | 8 | 7 | 10 | 6
test: «Hank the septopus and 2 normal octopuses, how many arms» | 23 | 24 | 21 | 22
test: In Moana, the giant crab Tamatoa covers his shell with shiny treasure to look fancy. Why do real decorator crabs cover their shells? | To hide from predators | To show off to other crabs on the reef | To catch the eye of a mate | To keep their shells warm
test: «4 stick insects on a branch, 6 legs each, how many legs» | 24 | 16 | 20 | 28
test: «2 tigers in the long grass, how many stripy tails n ears» | 6 | 4 | 8 | 10
test: «tigers are bright orange, they'd never hide from a deer, obviously» What is right? | Deer cannot see orange well, so the tiger blends into the grass for them | Right, deer always see them | Tigers are invisible to everyone | Deer only see orange
test: What would happen to a pale moth on a soot-blackened tree? | Birds would spot it more easily | It would turn black at once to match the bark | It would become invisible | Birds would ignore it
test: «that branch has a broken bit that just opened its eyes!!» What is it most likely? | A potoo pretending to be a stump | A stick insect | A tree fungus | A sleeping squirrel curled up on a branch
test: What would happen if a decorator crab moved from a sponge reef to a seaweed patch? | It would change its decorations to match | It would stay dressed in the same sponge forever | It would shed its shell | It would turn green by itself
test: «flounder's face is all wonky, was it born like that?» What is right? | It hatched with eyes on both sides, and one moved over as it grew | Yes, flounders hatch wonky | It got squashed flat by a passing whale, and one eye was pushed over | It is two fish stuck together
test: «saw a pink orchid eat a fly, plants that eat flies everywhere» What is more likely? | It was an orchid mantis, an insect that looks like a flower | Orchids always eat flies | The fly was sleeping | The flower was made of plastic, and the fly got stuck to the glue
exam: Identify the means by which the cuttlefish alters its colouration. | Specialised skin cells expand and contract to display different pigments | It ingests pigmented food | It applies its own ink to its skin | It replaces its skin entirely
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the camouflage of the tiger in relation to deer? | Its stripes disrupt its outline, and deer, unable to perceive orange well, see it as blending with vegetation | It relies upon remaining entirely invisible | Deer perceive orange more vividly than human beings | The stripes serve exclusively to regulate temperature
exam: Ascertain the consequence for moths of the darkening of tree bark by industrial soot. | Dark moths survived in greater numbers than pale ones | Pale moths survived in greater numbers on the darkened trees | All moths perished without distinction | The moths were unaffected
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the potoo. | It resembles a dead stump and perceives its surroundings through notches in its closed eyelids | It conceals itself beneath the sea floor | It alters its colour within one second | It is a species of moth
